{% extends "base.html.jinja" %}
{% import "macros.jinja" as m %}

{% block title %}Request a record — Significance{% endblock %}
{% block topline_meta %}Ask for a claim-state record{% endblock %}

{#
  Two actions, no backend. The site is static and stays static: a form posting
  somewhere would need a server, a store, and a policy about what happens to
  what people type into it, none of which this project has. A GitHub issue and
  an email are both places where the request is visible to the person making it
  and can be withdrawn by them.

  The GitHub link carries `?template=`, which opens the form at
  .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/record-request.yml with its three fields already
  labelled. The mailto carries the same three fields as a body, so somebody who
  will not open a GitHub account is not asked to answer a different set of
  questions.
#}
{% set repo = site.get('repository_url') %}
{% set template_name = site.get('record_request_template', 'record-request.yml') %}
{% set issue_url = (repo ~ '/issues/new?template=' ~ template_name) if repo else None %}
{% set contact = site.get('contact_email')|safe_email %}
{% set mail_subject = 'Record request' %}
{% set mail_body -%}
Link to the claim:

Your role (author / reader / other):

What you want tracked:

{% endset %}

{% block content %}

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Request a record</p>
  <h1 class="claim-text">Ask for a claim to be tracked.</h1>
  <p class="prose">
    A record says what was claimed, who asserted it, what evidence exists, and
    what nobody has checked yet. It does not say whether the mathematics is
    right, and nothing in it ever will. If that is the thing you need, this is
    how to ask for it — you do not need to know any of this project's
    vocabulary to file the request. Putting it in plain words is our job.
  </p>
</section>

{% if site.get('request_turnaround') %}
<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">What happens after you ask</p>
  <p class="prose">
    Requests are answered within {{ site.request_turnaround }}. That is a
    promise about the reply, not about the record: the answer may well be that
    no record will be created, and it will say why. A request that goes
    nowhere is closed without a public judgement attached to it.
  </p>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Before you ask — records about other people</p>
  <p class="prose">
    A record about a living person's claim is filed only after that person has
    been contacted. If they decline, no record is filed, and nothing anywhere
    in this project records that they were asked or that they said no: there is
    no decline list, in public, in private, or in maintainer notes. Aggregate
    counts of outreach may be published; individuals who said no never appear
    in them. This mirrors the outreach rules in the project's moderation
    policy, and it applies to requests as much as to anything the maintainers
    initiate themselves.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    Asking about your own claim skips all of that. You are the person who would
    otherwise be contacted.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Three things we need</p>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>The claim</dt>
    <dd>A link to where it actually lives — paper, preprint, repository, announcement, post. The most stable one, if it is in several places.</dd>
    <dt>Your role</dt>
    <dd>Author, reader, or something else. This changes what happens next rather than how the request is ranked: an author can attest to their own work, and a reader asking about someone else's claim starts the contact step above.</dd>
    <dt>What to track</dt>
    <dd>In your own words. Which statement matters, what evidence you know of, what you think nobody has checked.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Action — open an issue</p>
  {% if issue_url %}
  <p class="prose">
    {{ m.external_link(issue_url, 'Open a prefilled record request on GitHub') }} —
    the three fields above, already labelled. Needs a GitHub account.
  </p>
  {% else %}
  <p class="prose">
    No repository URL is configured for this build, so the prefilled issue link
    is not shown. It is a one-line value in <code>data/site.yaml</code>.
  </p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section class="band">
  <p class="lbl">Action — send it by email</p>
  {% if contact %}
  <p class="prose">
    <a href="mailto:{{ contact }}?subject={{ mail_subject|urlencode }}&amp;body={{ mail_body|urlencode }}">Email the same three fields</a>,
    already templated in the message body. No account needed.
  </p>
  {% else %}
  <p class="prose">
    No contact address is configured for this build, so there is no email link
    to offer. Rather than link somewhere that goes nowhere, here is the message
    to send once an address exists — the same three fields, in the same order:
  </p>
  <pre class="mail-template">Subject: {{ mail_subject }}

{{ mail_body }}</pre>
  <p class="meta">
    Setting <code>contact_email</code> in <code>data/site.yaml</code> turns this
    into a mailto link. It is deliberately unset rather than guessed: a link to
    an invented address would let somebody believe they had asked when nobody
    received anything.
  </p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section class="band what-this-does-not-establish">
  <p class="lbl">What filing a request does not do</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It does not commit anyone to creating a record.</li>
    <li>A request that goes nowhere is closed without a public judgement attached to it.</li>
    <li>A record, if one is created, still asserts nothing about whether the mathematics is right.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

{% endblock %}
